{"name":"Arrow Rock","hex":"#a28440","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"arrow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A projectile consisting of a shaft, a point and a tail with stabilizing fins that is shot from a bow.","A sign or symbol used to indicate a direction (e.g.→)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move swiftly and directly (like an arrow).","To let fly swiftly and directly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/arrow"},{"term":"rock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A formation of minerals, specifically:\n  The naturally occurring aggregate of solid mineral matter that constitutes a significant part of the earth's crust.","The naturally occurring aggregate of solid mineral matter that constitutes a significant part of the earth's crust."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pelt with rocks; to stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rock"}],"entries":[{"title":"Arrow Rock (New Zealand)","blocks":[{"text":"Arrow Rock is a small island in Tasman Bay / Te Tai-o-Aorere, off the coast of Nelson, New Zealand. There is a narrow channel between the rock and Haulashore Island. Situated within Nelson Harbour, Arrow Rock was site of the Fifeshire wreck in 1842, which gave the rock its secondary name. There are a number of spotted shags who live on the rock."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Port_Nelson_with_Arrow_Rock.jpg/330px-Port_Nelson_with_Arrow_Rock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrow_Rock_(New_Zealand)","wikidata":"Q4796130"},{"title":"Arrow Rocks","blocks":[{"text":"The Arrow Rocks are islands that surround Oruatemanu Island of the Tauranga Bay in New Zealand, about 40 kilometres (25 mi) north of the Bay of Islands. One of the islands looks like an arrow or a bird's beak."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrow_Rocks","wikidata":"Q32120637"},{"title":"Arrow","bloc